#!/usr/bin/env -S uv run --quiet --script
# /// script
# dependencies = ["boto3", "pyyaml"]
# ///

"""
ant-ingest — end-to-end ingestion from S3 __incoming/ to the catalog.

Workflow (default subcommand):
  1. Scan s3://<bucket>/__incoming/ → group by data_project (+ optional
     single-segment subfolder).
  2. For each h5ad in __incoming/, pull it and its `<basename>.h5ad.mus`
     sidecar into  data/h5adstore/.staging/<dp>/[<sub>/]
  3. Verify the pulled h5ad's sha256 against `sha256=` in the sidecar.
     Mismatch → mark dataset failed (refuse to ingest).
  4. Check the local catalog (data/h5adstore/<dp>/[<sub>/]<basename>.h5ad):
       absent       → promote staging → catalog
       same sha     → skip the h5ad copy, proceed to metadata merge
       different sha→ STOP this dataset (verbose diff; user must resolve)
  5. Merge the freshly-pulled sidecar with the catalog sidecar via
     `lib.sidecar_merge` (KEY=VALUE rules; conflicts warn but don't abort
     — they are recorded in the run report).
  6. Promote any  __incoming/<dp>/static/<basename>  files to
     data/h5adstore/<dp>/static/<basename> (sha-compare; identical →
     ignore, different → warn).
  7. Run `ant-h5ad-check`, `ant-convert`, `ant-duckdb-check` on the
     newly-promoted h5ad. Any failure aborts THIS dataset (others continue).
  8. Push the catalog h5ad + sidecar to s3://<bucket>/<dp>/[<sub>/]
     (catalog root, NOT __incoming/). On success, re-stamp the sidecar's
     s3_* block so it reflects the catalog key + new etag/uploaded_at.
  9. Record the consumed __incoming/ keys + staging files in
     data/h5adstore/.staging/_pending_prune.json so `ant-ingest prune`
     can clean them later.

At end of run, print a per-dataset status table + a "Pending cleanup"
section with concrete next commands.

Subcommands:
  ant-ingest             # default: pull + run end-to-end
  ant-ingest --dry-run   # show what would happen, no destructive ops
  ant-ingest --filter <data_project>  # restrict scan to one project
  ant-ingest prune              # remove staging + S3 __incoming/ keys
  ant-ingest prune --dry-run    # preview only
  ant-ingest prune --include-catalog-orphans
                                # additionally remove S3 catalog files
                                # that no longer exist locally

Non-destructive guarantee: `ant-ingest` (without `prune`) never deletes
anything on S3. `ant-ingest prune` is the only command that calls S3
DeleteObject.
"""
